    "created_at": "2022-07-20T13:50:33Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-07-20T13:50:33Z",
    "body": "Some quick notes from various twitter threads with devs using it, specifically for the case where no-cache prefetches are available for one-time-use from a memory cache (and it isn't just a priority setting backed by the normal disk cache):\r\n\r\n- Some way to limit the duration of the usability of the no-cache resource in the HTTP response.  Like max-age but if having a max-age on no-cache is a problem, a different value like max-fresh or something (may require HTTP spec changes).\r\n- Same, but from the link tag side (client-controlled reduction of the timer). In the case of a \"reactive prefetch\" implementation where the next page HTML is prefetched on hover of a link and the expectation is that it will be used right away, a very short max-age is more appropriate than 5 minutes.\r\n- Have Vary: work with the memory cache so that if a login (or similar) barrier is passed, stale content is not served.\r\n- A way to specify individual cookies that vary applies to for the cookie case (this has been a general HTTP ask for a while and has been discussed before - not sure the current state).",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
